Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// SMILES acrylate generation CSD - deep diagnosis and fix.
//
// Root cause analysis:
// - All prior attempts produce "<<C>>" - a single carbon atom "C" inside the span.
// - The model (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct) on the prompt "acrylates" immediately picks
//   "C" as the first SMILES token, which is parser-valid and complete (single atom
//   is a valid SMILES), so the span closes immediately.
// - The SMILES parser accepts "C" as a complete parse, so CloseSpanIfComplete
//   triggers after just one token.
//
// Key insight: we need to PREVENT early closure on trivially complete but
// semantically wrong SMILES. The acrylate scaffold requires at minimum the
// pattern C=CC(=O)O. We need to force the model to generate this scaffold
// before we allow closure.
//
// Strategy:
// 1. Use AppendTaskGuidance with very explicit SMILES scaffold instruction.
// 2. Do NOT open the span immediately. First, let the model generate a few
//    unconstrained tokens to get past any immediate "C" tendency.
// 3. When we open the span, use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep to avoid repeating
//    tokens already in generated (discourages single-char outputs).
// 4. Use AdaptiveConstrainedStep with a higher narrow threshold (50) so that
//    group boosts apply more broadly.
// 5. Critically: do NOT check CloseSpanIfComplete eagerly. Only close after
//    we've accumulated enough constrained tokens (e.g., at least 5 tokens in
//    currentConstrainedOut). This prevents premature closure on "C".
// 6. Use a minimum constrained token count before allowing close.
//
// The minimum-token guard is the key fix: by requiring at least 5 tokens in
// currentConstrainedOut before closure, we force the model past single-atom
// trivially-complete SMILES.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity:
//   1. After EnterObservedConstrainedSpan or OpenConstrainedSpan:
//      currentConstrainedOut := [] which satisfies parser.IsValidPrefix([])
//      by the precondition.
