Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// Root cause analysis:
// - The model generates <<C>> - a single carbon atom in the constrained span.
// - "C" is a complete SMILES (valid single atom), so parser.IsCompletePrefix returns true immediately.
// - Our strategy closes the span immediately after "C".
// - Visible span token length avg: 1.00 - confirms this.
//
// The core problem: SMILES grammar accepts single atoms as complete prefixes.
// We need the model to generate a full acrylate like "C=CC(=O)OCC" not just "C".
//
// Key insight from diagnostics:
// - "Tokens before first visible open: avg 0.00" - we open the span with 0 unconstrained tokens
// - "entered_constrained_mode_too_early: 50 examples"
// - The model has no context about what SMILES to generate before entering constrained mode
//
// Fix: Use UnconstrainedChunk to generate some preamble tokens first, letting the model
// "warm up" and see prompt context. Then enter constrained mode.
// BUT also: we must force longer constrained generation by NOT closing on first IsCompletePrefix.
//
// The real question: why does the model output just "C"?
// - The model tokenizes SMILES as individual characters: "C", "=", "C", "(", etc.
// - After "C", the parser says IsCompletePrefix=true (valid SMILES).
// - Our strategy closes immediately.
//
// Solution: Track constrained token count and only close when we've accumulated
// enough tokens OR when EOS is received AND IsCompletePrefix.
//
// But there's a subtlety: once IsCompletePrefix is true, we cannot call AppendConstrainedToken
// (requires !IsCompletePrefix). So we need to use CloseConstrainedSpan when complete.
//
// Alternative: Use SafeRepetitionPenaltyStep which generates the full SMILES as a unit
// in some tokenizers. But in Qwen, SMILES tokens are character-by-character.
//
// Better approach: Use TopValidCandidates to peek and bias, or use
// AdaptiveConstrainedStepWithPenalties to penalize short completions.
//
// Actually the real fix: we need the SMILES parser to produce longer outputs.
// The SMILES grammar accepts "C" as complete but also accepts "C=CC(=O)OCC" as complete.
// We need to steer the model to generate the longer form.
//
// Strategy:
// 1. Use AppendTaskGuidance with very explicit acrylate SMILES example.
// 2. Use UnconstrainedChunk to warm up with a few tokens (let model generate text about 
//    what molecule it will make) before entering constrained span.
// 3. Once in constrained span, DON'T close until we have sufficient content:
//    - Track steps in constrained span
//    - Only allow closure after minConstrainedTokens steps consumed
//    - If IsCompletePrefix but not enough tokens: use AdaptiveConstrainedStep to 
//      try to extend - but we cannot append if IsCompletePrefix is true!
//
// The fundamental issue: if the parser says IsCompletePrefix=true after "C", we
// cannot extend with AppendConstrainedToken. The only solution is to NOT enter
// constrained mode until the model is already "committed" to outputting a longer SMILES.
//
// Alternative approach: Use the unconstrained chunk to let the model write the SMILES
// freely, then extract and validate. But we need parser control for syntax guarantees.
//
// BEST APPROACH: 
// 1. Do NOT use visible << >> at all (hidden constrained mode).
//    - Start generation with ConstrainedStep immediately (no OpenConstrainedSpan)
//    - This means we're in constrained mode from step 1 with insideConstrainedOut=false
//    - Wait... but insideConstrainedOut=false means we're NOT in constrained mode per the state.
//
// Actually let me re-read the task contract:
// "Use the hidden parser-guided constrained chunk for that SMILES token sequence"
// "Answer contract: single SMILES string"
// "Contains << >>: optional"
//
// So we DON'T need visible << >>. We should use hidden constrained mode.
// The answer is extracted by "hidden_or_task_extractor".
//
// How to do hidden constrained generation:
// - We call ConstrainedStep directly with currentConstrained = [] 
// - We track currentConstrained ourselves
// - We do NOT call OpenConstrainedSpan (which would add visible <<)
// - We append tokens to generated without the << >> delimiters
// - The evaluator extracts the answer from the text directly
//
// But the helpers.AppendConstrainedToken adds to both generated and currentConstrained.
// And helpers.OpenConstrainedSpan adds visible "<<".
//
// For hidden mode: we want to append tokens to generated WITHOUT visible delimiters.
// Looking at the API - there's no "hidden constrained append" helper.
// But we can use EnterObservedConstrainedSpan after OpenConstrainedSpan... no.
//
// Actually: We want to call ConstrainedStep + AppendConstrainedToken but without 
// the visible << >> wrapper. The AppendConstrainedToken adds the token to BOTH
// generated and currentConstrained. So generated will contain the raw SMILES tokens.
// The << >> are only added by OpenConstrainedSpan and CloseConstrainedSpan.
//
// So the plan:
// 1. DO NOT call OpenConstrainedSpan
// 2. Manually set insideConstrainedOut = true and currentConstrainedOut = []
//    - This is valid since parser.IsValidPrefix([]) holds by precondition
// 3. Call ConstrainedStep (or AdaptiveConstrainedStep) with the current state
// 4. Call AppendConstrainedToken to add token to generated (no visible << >>)
// 5. DO NOT call CloseConstrainedSpan (would add visible >>)
// 6. At the end, insideConstrainedOut may still be true with a valid prefix
//
// Wait - but the invariants require insideConstrainedOut ==> |currentConstrainedOut| <= |generated|
// If we set insideConstrainedOut = true with currentConstrainedOut = [] initially,
// and generated = generatedPrefix, then |[]| = 0 <= |generatedPrefix|, so OK.
//
// And the ensures require:
// insideConstrainedOut ==> parser.IsValidPrefix(currentConstrainedOut) ✓
//
// So the generated output will just be the raw SMILES tokens without delimiters.
// The evaluator's "hidden_or_task_extractor" should find them.
